y's missing and  murdered indigenous women and girls West  block hosts Mercedes Stevenson sat down  with Cassidy Caron metis National  Council president to talk about this and  the other issues her communities are  facing  Cassidy I was really struck watching the  tragedy unfolding in Winnipeg of the  state that we still face for indigenous  women in this country and the attention  that that their family members and  daughters were able to bring by being so  strong and so articulate may make the  difference there but there are many many  more women who are Vanishing and who  have died and have been murdered and and  it just sort of goes as a blip on the  radar and as long as I've been in Ottawa  people have been talking about this  issue  why do you think it is that it feels  like we're on a treadmill with it and  and there's commitment after commitment  at various levels of government uh but  then we continue to see it be this  incredible tragedy for indigenous  Canadians and for all Canadians that  this is happening  such a massive issue that permeates  every corner of our society and I think  that because it is such a massive issue  it paralyzes so many people they don't  know what to do but you're absolutely  right people have been talking about  this crisis that we face in Canada for  years now and it's so unfair to family  members to continue to have to go to the  media and tell their stories over and  over again for it only to pick up steam  for a couple of weeks maybe a month and  then for it to drop off until again it  becomes a big news story we've seen this  happen time and time again in Canada  where indigenous people are telling  their stories they're telling their  truths you know with the Royal  Commission on Aboriginal people The  Truth and Reconciliation Commission the  inquiry into missing and murdered  indigenous women and girls and even most  recently as a senate report on the  forced sterilization of indigenous women  and girls  people are telling their stories and  it's time for Canadians it's time for  politicians at all levels to listen and  to really dedicate the time the  resources and just the care to create  the change so that we can move forward  in a good way  you can catch a Mercedes full interview  with Cassidy Caron tomorrow on the west  block right here on global foreign  Discovery decades in the making next the  amazing coincidence that reunited a  Toronto woman with a cherished  photograph  [Music]  during a visit to the Art Gallery of  Ontario a woman noticed someone very  familiar in an exhibit her father as  Brittany Rosen reports the discovery was  not only a surprise to her but also to  her dad  not long after Jeanette Robinson Daley  stepped foot into this exhibit at the  Art Gallery of Ontario just a half hour  before they closed She was drawn to a  very familiar face asked my dad in a sea  of photos on display there was her  father Prince Robinson there was a  couple young people here in Millennials  around and I was like hey this is my dad  they're just like okay whatever but at  that time that meant so much to me this  is the old apartment Robinson Daly says  she recognized everything in the photo  taken by her mother more than 50 years  ago from the apartment near Christy  Pitts Park to the man holding the  trumpet she immediately called her dad I  said I saw your picture and the art  gallery and he's like what I said yes  you you're holding a trumpet and he was  he said tears came to his eyes at that  time it's  befota was found somewhere in California  and to this day Prince still has the  Polaroid camera that captured it the  once forgotten moment now being  displayed in the ago's what matters most  exhibit which highlights everyday  moments of black family life the  creators of this exhibit went through  thousands of photos that were lost all  over North America and then found they  then selected the 500 that are on these  walls and given the circumstances of  their Discovery 